from gklib import L, S, Q
L("tech","how-computers-count-binary","8-10","How Computers Count: Binary","Computers use only two digits, 0 and 1. Learn to count and write numbers their way.","💻",[
 S("Two digits only","Inside a computer there are billions of tiny switches that can be on or off. | We write 'on' as 1 and 'off' as 0, so computers use only two digits. | This way of writing numbers is called binary.",["Switches: on or off","1 and 0","Binary"],"binary-counter"),
 S("Place values","In our normal numbers each place is worth ten times the one to its right. | In binary each place is worth two times the one to its right: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 and so on. | The binary number 101 means one four, no twos and one one, which is 5.",["Places are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16","101 in binary is 5"],"binary-counter"),
 S("Count to ten","Counting in binary goes: 0, 1, 10, 11, 100, 101, 110, 111, 1000, 1001, 1010. | So 1010 is ten, because it has one eight and one two. | Try finding your age in binary.",["1010 is ten"],"binary-counter"),
 S("From bits to everything","One binary digit is called a bit, and eight bits make a byte. | Letters, pictures, music and videos are all stored as long strings of bits. | The letter A, for example, is stored as the number 65, which is 01000001 in binary.",["Bit and byte","Everything is bits"]),
],facts=["A one-megabyte song file holds about eight million bits.","Computers are fast because switches can flip billions of times every second.","The idea of binary was written about by the Indian scholar Pingala around two thousand years ago and later by Gottfried Leibniz."],
quiz=[Q("How many digits does binary use?",["Two","Ten","Eight"],0,"Binary uses 0 and 1."),
Q("What is binary 101 in ordinary numbers?",["3","5","7"],1,"4 plus 0 plus 1 is 5."),
Q("How many bits are in a byte?",["4","8","16"],1,"A byte is 8 bits.")],tags=["binary","computers"],notes="Pingala's binary-like system of syllable patterns is a historical note; see a history of mathematics for details.")

L("tech","how-the-internet-works","11-13","How the Internet Works","How a message crosses the world in a fraction of a second: packets, cables and addresses.","🌐",[
 S("A network of networks","The internet is a huge network of connected computers all over the world. | Your phone connects to a nearby router, the router connects to your internet provider, and providers connect to each other. | Most of the world's internet traffic travels through cables, many of them under the sea.",["A network of networks","Many cables are under the sea"]),
 S("Addresses","Every device on the internet has an address, called an IP address. | We use easy names such as websites, and a system called DNS works like a phone book that turns the name into the address. | That way you can type a name instead of a number.",["IP address","DNS is a phone book"]),
 S("Packets","When you send a photo, it is broken into many small pieces called packets. | Each packet travels by the best path it can find, and they may take different routes. | At the other end they are put back together in the right order.",["Data is split into packets","Different routes","Put together again"]),
 S("Staying safe","Not everything on the internet is true or safe. | Use strong passwords, do not share personal details with strangers and ask an adult if something feels wrong. | Check a fact in more than one reliable place before you believe it.",["Be careful","Check facts","Ask an adult"]),
],facts=["The World Wide Web, the pages we browse, is only one part of the internet. Email and video calls use the internet too.","Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web in 1989.","Light signals inside glass fibre cables carry the data very fast."],
quiz=[Q("What turns a website name into an address?",["DNS","A router","A battery"],0,"DNS is like the internet's phone book."),
Q("What are the small pieces of data called?",["Packets","Pages","Parcels"],0,"Data travels as packets."),
Q("Where do many internet cables run?",["Under the sea","Only in the sky","Only in houses"],0,"Many cables are laid on the sea floor.")],tags=["internet","network"])

L("tech","what-is-artificial-intelligence","11-13","What Is Artificial Intelligence?","How a machine can learn patterns from examples, and what it still cannot do.","🤖",[
 S("Learning from examples","Artificial intelligence, or AI, is computer software that can do tasks that usually need human thinking. | Most modern AI learns from many examples rather than following only fixed rules. | Show a program thousands of pictures of cats and dogs, and it learns the patterns that tell them apart.",["AI learns from examples","Finds patterns"]),
 S("Where you meet it","AI helps your phone recognise a voice, suggests videos, translates between languages and helps doctors study scans. | It is also used in maps to find the fastest way home. | Chat programs can write text because they have learned patterns from a huge amount of writing.",["Voice, translation, maps"]),
 S("What it cannot do","AI does not understand the world like a person does. | It can make mistakes, repeat unfair patterns found in its data, or say something that sounds sure but is wrong. | So people must check its work, especially for health, news and school facts.",["Can be wrong","May copy unfair patterns","Always check"]),
 S("You in charge","AI is a tool, and people decide how it is used. | Use it to learn, but do your own thinking and keep your own voice. | The best way to be ready for the future is to keep learning how to ask good questions.",["A tool, not a boss","Keep thinking for yourself"]),
],facts=["The term artificial intelligence was first used at a workshop at Dartmouth in 1956.","Modern AI needs large amounts of computing power and electricity.","A calculator is not usually called AI, because it follows fixed rules."],
quiz=[Q("How do many modern AI programs learn?",["From many examples","By magic","By eating data"],0,"They find patterns in lots of examples."),
Q("Which is true about AI answers?",["They are always right","They can be wrong","They never change"],1,"AI can make mistakes, so check them."),
Q("Who decides how AI is used?",["People","The computer alone","Nobody"],0,"People build and choose how to use AI.")],tags=["ai","machine learning"],asof="November 2026",notes="AI is changing quickly; this lesson explains the stable ideas only.")

L("money","what-is-money-and-how-banks-work","8-10","What Is Money? How Banks Work","From swapping goods to coins, notes and bank accounts, and what saving really means.","💰",[
 S("Before money","Long ago people swapped things they had for things they needed, which is called barter. | Barter is hard: what if the farmer wants shoes but the shoemaker does not want rice? | So people agreed to use something everyone accepts, and that became money.",["Barter swaps goods","Money solves the swap problem"]),
 S("What money does","Money lets us buy things, keeps value to use later and helps us compare prices. | In India the money is the rupee, written ₹, and the Reserve Bank of India issues the notes. | Today a lot of money is also digital, such as payments through UPI.",["Buy and compare","Stores value","The rupee"]),
 S("Banks","A bank keeps people's savings safe. | It also lends some of that money to others, such as people who want to start a shop, and charges them a little extra called interest. | It pays a smaller interest to the people who saved.",["Safe place to save","Lends money for interest"]),
 S("Needs, wants and saving","A need is something you must have, such as food, and a want is something nice to have, such as a toy. | A good habit is to save a part of any money you get before spending the rest. | Small savings grow when you keep adding to them.",["Needs and wants","Save first"]),
],facts=["The first coins were made in Lydia, in what is now Turkey, about 2,600 years ago.","Cowrie shells were used as money in many parts of the world, including India.","The Reserve Bank of India was founded in 1935."],
quiz=[Q("What is barter?",["Swapping goods without money","Printing notes","Saving in a bank"],0,"Barter is exchanging one thing for another."),
Q("What is interest?",["Extra money paid for borrowing or saving","A toy","A loan form"],0,"Interest is the price of using money."),
Q("Which is a need?",["Food","A new toy","A game"],0,"Food is a need.")],tags=["money","bank","saving"])

L("money","trade-and-how-prices-are-set","11-13","Trade and How Prices Are Set","Why countries trade, and how supply and demand set the price of a mango.","⚖️",[
 S("Why trade?","No country has everything. | India grows spices and tea, while some countries have oil or make advanced machines. | By trading, each place can sell what it does best and buy what it lacks.",["No one has everything","Trade shares skills and goods"]),
 S("Supply and demand","Supply is how much of something is for sale, and demand is how much people want to buy. | In mango season there are many mangoes, so the price falls. | Out of season there are few, and many people still want them, so the price goes up.",["Supply: how much there is","Demand: how much is wanted"]),
 S("Prices send signals","A high price tells sellers to bring more and tells buyers to use less. | A low price does the opposite. | That is how a market, a place where people trade, balances itself without anybody giving orders.",["Prices work as signals"]),
 S("Real life","Prices also depend on the cost of making and carrying a thing, taxes and quality. | Governments sometimes set limits on prices of important things like medicines. | Learning to compare prices helps you spend wisely.",["Costs, taxes, quality","Compare before buying"]),
],facts=["India is among the world's largest producers of milk, pulses and spices.","Ancient Tamil traders sold pepper, pearls and cloth to Rome more than 2,000 years ago.","Items that are rare and wanted by many, like gold, tend to have high prices."],
quiz=[Q("What happens to price when supply is high and demand is the same?",["It falls","It rises","It stays the same always"],0,"More supply means a lower price."),
Q("What is demand?",["How much people want to buy","How much is for sale","How much a thing costs to make"],0,"Demand is the amount wanted."),
Q("Why do countries trade?",["To get what they lack and sell what they have","Only to compete","Only for fun"],0,"Trade shares what each place has.")],tags=["trade","prices"])

L("green","climate-change-explained","11-13","Climate Change Explained","The greenhouse effect, what changes the climate and what people can do.","🌡️",[
 S("The blanket around Earth","Some gases in the air, like carbon dioxide and methane, act like a blanket that traps the Sun's heat. | This is the greenhouse effect, and a natural amount of it keeps Earth warm enough for life. | Without it Earth would be an icy planet.",["Greenhouse gases trap heat","A little is needed for life"]),
 S("Too much blanket","When we burn coal, oil and gas for power, vehicles and factories, extra carbon dioxide goes into the air. | Cutting down forests also adds carbon dioxide and removes trees that absorb it. | The extra gas traps more heat, so the planet's average temperature is rising.",["Burning fuels adds carbon dioxide","Forests absorb it"]),
 S("What changes","Scientists see ice melting, sea levels rising and more extreme weather such as heatwaves and heavy rain. | Coastal cities like Chennai face higher risks from rising seas and strong storms. | The pace and size of the changes depend on what the world does next.",["Melting ice","Rising seas","Extreme weather"]),
 S("What we can do","We can use less energy, switch to solar and wind power, plant trees and waste less food. | Walking, cycling or using public transport instead of cars helps. | Small choices add up when millions of people make them.",["Save energy","Clean power","Plant trees","Travel smart"]),
],facts=["Global average temperature has risen by about 1.1 to 1.2 degrees Celsius since the late 1800s.","India is investing a lot in solar power and has one of the world's largest solar programmes.","Trees take in carbon dioxide as they grow."],
quiz=[Q("Which gas is a main greenhouse gas?",["Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Helium"],0,"Carbon dioxide traps heat in the atmosphere."),
Q("Which action helps the climate?",["Planting trees","Burning more coal","Wasting food"],0,"Trees take in carbon dioxide."),
Q("Why do coastal cities worry about climate change?",["Sea levels are rising","More desert","Colder winters"],0,"Higher seas and stronger storms threaten coasts.")],tags=["climate","greenhouse"],asof="November 2026",notes="Temperature figures come from major scientific assessments (for example the IPCC) and are updated every few years.")

L("green","save-water-save-life","5-7","Save Water, Save Life","Where our water comes from and easy ways to use less of it.","🚰",[
 S("Water is precious","Plants, animals and people all need water to live. | Most of Earth's water is salty sea water that we cannot drink. | Only a small part is fresh water that we can use.",["All life needs water","Little fresh water"]),
 S("Where it comes from","Rain fills rivers, lakes and the ground. | We take water from them, clean it and send it to taps. | In Chennai and many places in India, people have also used tanks, ponds and wells for storing rain.",["Rain fills rivers and lakes","Ponds and wells store water"]),
 S("Small steps","Turn the tap off while brushing your teeth. | Fix a dripping tap, because drops add up to many litres. | Use a bucket instead of a hose for washing, and finish your drink instead of pouring it away.",["Close the tap","Fix drips","Use a bucket"]),
 S("Catch the rain","Rainwater harvesting means collecting rain from rooftops and letting it soak into the ground or fill a tank. | Tamil Nadu was one of the first states in India to make rainwater harvesting compulsory in buildings, in 2003. | This helps keep wells and groundwater full.",["Collect rainwater","Tamil Nadu: compulsory since 2003"]),
],facts=["A dripping tap can waste thousands of litres of water in a year.","Many old temple towns in Tamil Nadu have big tanks that fill with rain.","About two thirds of your body weight is water."],
quiz=[Q("Which is a good habit?",["Close the tap while brushing","Leave the tap running","Wash with a hose for long"],0,"Closing the tap saves water."),
Q("What is rainwater harvesting?",["Collecting rain to use or store","Making rain","Selling rain"],0,"It means catching rain from roofs and storing it or letting it soak into the ground."),
Q("What kind of water can people drink?",["Fresh clean water","Sea water","Muddy water"],0,"We drink fresh water that has been cleaned.")],tags=["water","rainwater"])
